  "analysis": {
    "why_you_should_care": "Language implementation is where theory stops being optional. Parsing is the tutorial. Semantics is the product.",
    "what_happened": "An independent compiler project published a self-hosted-or-nearly-self-hosted language with an unusual type system and its own IR.",
    "how_it_works": "Source \u2192 parser \u2192 typed AST \u2192 IR \u2192 backend (often LLVM). The interesting choices are in type inference, effects, and what they refused to inherit from C.",
    "under_the_hood": "Look at how they represent types, how they lower generics, and whether the backend is a real ISA or a detour through LLVM.",
    "why_they_built_it": "Existing languages encoded someone else's compromises.",
    "what_was_difficult": "Bootstrapping, error messages, and the moment the type checker becomes slower than the user's patience.",
    "performance": "The available sources do not establish this.",
    "trade_offs": "Novelty versus ecosystem. A beautiful IR with no libraries is still a beautiful IR.",
    "what_is_surprising": "They shipped enough of a standard library that the language is not only a blog post.",
    "quick_read": "A compiler from scratch, with a type system that has opinions.",
